  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Nobody in East Africa gets straight to business. You greet first, ask how things are, and only then ask for directions or the price of mangoes.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Habari?", "base": "How are you? (literally “news?”)" },
      { "target": "Nzuri, asante.", "base": "Fine, thank you." },
      { "target": "Mambo? Poa!", "base": "What’s up? Cool! (between friends)" },
      { "target": "Shikamoo.", "base": "My respects (to an elder)" },
      { "target": "Marahaba.", "base": "Thank you, I accept (the elder’s reply)" },
      { "target": "Jina lako nani?", "base": "What is your name?" },
      { "target": "Jina langu ni Amani.", "base": "My name is Amani." },
      { "target": "Ninatoka Arusha.", "base": "I come from Arusha." },
      { "target": "Karibu sana!", "base": "You’re very welcome!" },
      { "target": "Kwaheri, tutaonana.", "base": "Goodbye, see you later." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "news-greetings",
        "title": "Every greeting asks for news",
        "body": [
          "**Habari** means “news”, so **Habari?** is “any news?”. Add the time of day: **Habari za asubuhi?** (news of the morning?), **Habari za mchana?** (news of the afternoon?).",
          "The polite answer is always good news first: **Nzuri**, **Salama** (peaceful) or **Njema** (good). If something is wrong, you say so after."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ni-and-names",
        "title": "Ni: one little word for am, is and are",
        "body": [
          "**Ni** links two things for every person: **Mimi ni Emma** (I am Emma), **Wewe ni nani?** (who are you?), **Jina langu ni Amani** (my name is Amani).",
          "“My” and “your” come after the noun and agree with it: **jina langu**, **jina lako**. You’ll see them change with other nouns in the next unit."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "say-every-letter",
        "title": "Say it as it’s written",
        "body": [
          "Swahili spelling is regular: every letter is pronounced, and the vowels **a e i o u** sound like in Spanish or Italian. The stress almost always falls on the second-to-last syllable: a-SAN-te, ka-RI-bu, kwa-HE-ri."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Shikamoo for elders",
        "body": [
          "In Tanzania especially, young people greet anyone clearly older with **Shikamoo**, and the elder answers **Marahaba**. It is never said to someone younger, and saying it to a friend your own age is a joke."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Karibu means you are expected",
        "body": [
          "You will hear **Karibu** everywhere: at a door, in a shop, after “thank you”. A guest is usually offered a seat and something to drink before anything else is said."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
